Requests 5414 qualifies under the fourth, explicitly versioned ARM environment. Both controls complete all 131 designated checks: the unfixed source has 130 passes and the intended failure, while the gold source has 131 passes and the native grader marks it resolved. No model inference was performed.

This is an independent ARM environment qualification for the previously nominated Requests 5414 instance. It performs no model inference and does not enroll a trajectory or compare neurosymbolic methods. The original gold and test patches, test IDs, evaluation script and raw logs remain in curator custody; all public results contain infrastructure, status counts and identities only.

The pinned base source is `39d0fdd9096f7dceccbc8f82e1eda7dd64717a8e`. The dataset's separate environment-setup metadata is `a1a6a549a0143d9b32717dbe3d75cd543ae5a4f6`; these are preserved as distinct fields. The official task Dockerfile explicitly checks out the base source. Its public environment.yml is byte-identical to the Requests 6028 resource, but that identity alone did not make the first environment adequate for this earlier task.

Every profile retains Python 3.9.20 on the same immutable ARM base, exact source and hidden patch bytes, and the ordinary unmodified SWE-bench 5.0.2 grader imported through its real package initializer. Only the four exact conda-activation lines are removed from the original evaluation script. The grading requirement is a nonempty complete designated set, all designated gold checks passing, and the designated regression actually failing unfixed. Process exit zero is never treated as evidence that checks passed. Source, script, dataset/task reconciliation, image, package metadata, scorer and loaded native module hashes are retained separately for each attempted profile.

All four attempts remain evidence:

| Profile | Environment | Result |
|---|---|---|
| run-v1 | Original 13 Python pins; Docker network disabled | Both controls completed 131 designated checks. Unfixed: 126 pass, 5 fail. Gold: 127 pass, 4 fail. Gold remained unresolved. Both also had 158 errors and one XFAIL outside the designated set. Unqualified. |
| run-v2 | Public requirements-dev bounds, including local pytest-httpbin and trustme; original 12 non-pytest pins retained; default Docker bridge planned | Build failed before either control: Jinja2 imported removed MarkupSafe soft_unicode. |
| run-v3 | run-v2 plus MarkupSafe 2.0.1 | Build succeeded; both controls completed zero designated checks. Independent public pytest --version reproduced pytest-cov 6.2.0's wrapper hook incompatibility with frozen pluggy 1.0.0. Unqualified. |
| run-v4 | run-v3 plus pytest-cov 4.1.0 and passing public startup preflight | Both controls complete 131 designated checks; gold resolves and unfixed fails the intended check. Qualified. |

The development dependency profile uses the public requirements-dev constraint `pytest<=6.2.5`, overriding environment.yml's conflicting pytest 7.4.4 pin. The exact resolved pytest version is 6.2.5. MarkupSafe 2.0.1 addresses a public compatibility break: [upstream removed soft_unicode in 2.1.0](https://markupsafe.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/changes/#version-2-1-0). The final profile pins pytest-cov 4.1.0 and adds a public pytest --version build preflight. The [upstream Pluggy documentation](https://github.com/pytest-dev/pluggy/blob/main/docs/index.rst) identifies wrapper hooks as a later addition than the retained Pluggy 1.0.0.

The first offline profile's generic setup diagnostics identified absent trustme, which the public development requirements declare, plus network-unreachable errors in the four preserved designated failures. The native upstream container creation uses Docker's default network; the initial `none` setting was a stricter development choice. Later profiles enable ordinary bridge networking for the unchanged setup and checks and install the public local httpbin fixtures. No test, failure criterion or expected result is changed to accommodate the port.

Raw logs contain binary bytes from the original git show diagnostic against the shallow checkout. The [encoding addendum](../ENCODING_ADDENDUM.md) establishes the byte origin and keeps this source-history difference visible. Whole-log exception counts from early heuristics are not used to identify startup causes because that diagnostic contains source text. Later startup classification is confined to the test-output region and is independently reproduced using public dependency commands.

The shared ordinary grader environment has real Docker SDK, datasets and optional cloud dependencies installed; it uses no initializer bypass or sys.modules stubs. Dependency resolution, network availability and system stack differ from the published x86 image. This qualified ARM control pair establishes this explicitly versioned designated-check environment only, with separate candidate-scorer integrity and trajectory enrollment still required.

The final image is `sha256:18e7ce03a99952762ec748a2fbad9d44e0859e8a636818d5ee02f280867f0a3e`. It has 38 distributions; all package versions remain identical after each original setup/check execution. The image metadata and resolved third-party lock preserve those versions. A future rebuild is a new image requiring identity checking and requalification; the Docker recipe itself does not pin every apt repository state. The matched controls use default bridge networking, two CPUs, 2 GiB RAM, 128 process limit and a 180-second absolute evaluation timeout.

Full parsed outputs comprise 288 passes, one failure and one XFAIL unfixed, versus 289 passes and one XFAIL gold. No setup ERROR, missing designated check, skipped designated check, timeout or OOM occurred. The XFAIL lies outside the designated set. Measured execution times are 35.85 and 35.72 seconds. The original script supplies no test-exit marker, so that field remains null in both controls; native status parsing and the complete designated set determine the grade.

The final artifact audit passes 189 source, custody, decoder, grading and package checks. Earlier profile audits pass 183, 132 and 187 checks respectively while explicitly retaining their unqualified outcomes. These audits perform no new control execution. The service contract names the qualified image and scorer profile, but has no candidate-scoring entrypoint yet: a separate candidate adapter and integrity qualification remain required before model or integrated-controller inference.
